  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v1.1.0:\n\t\t\t- Requires EventFlux 2.1.1 or later; handlers use the 2.x signature Handle(TEvent, CancellationToken)\n\t\t\t- Fixed AddRedisEventQueue failing with EventFlux 2.x (ArgumentException: At least one assembly must be provided); the assemblies you pass are now forwarded to AddEventBus\n\t\t\t- Fixed the worker failing with MissingMethodException on EventFlux 2.x; the host stopping token is passed to handlers\n\t\t\t- Fixed handlers being invoked twice when they were registered both by AddEventBus and by AddRedisEventQueue\n\t\t\t- Targets net8.0, net9.0 and net10.0; net6.0 and net7.0 are dropped because EventFlux 2.x does not support them\n\t\t\t- Microsoft.Extensions.* dependencies follow each target's major version (net8.0 uses 8.x), which fixes CS1705 in net8.0 applications\n\t\t\t- Added IRedisStreamPublisher.PublishAsync<TEvent>(TEvent, CancellationToken) that writes the same type name and JSON payload the worker reads\n\t\t\t- Failed messages are no longer acknowledged and deleted: they stay pending, are retried after RetryIdleTime and are moved to a dead-letter stream after MaxDeliveryAttempts deliveries (new RedisStreamOptions: EnableRetry, MaxDeliveryAttempts, RetryIdleTime, DeadLetterStreamName)\n\t\t\t- Fixed entries being lost for other consumer groups: an acknowledged entry is deleted only once every consumer group of the stream has read and acknowledged it (new RedisStreamOptions.DeleteProcessedEntries, default true; false keeps all entries)\n\t\t\t- Fixed the worker stopping the application when Redis is unavailable for more than a few seconds or when the stream or consumer group is deleted; the worker now retries with a backoff of up to 30 seconds and recreates the consumer group\n\t\t\t- The application now starts while Redis is unavailable and connects once Redis is reachable; add abortConnect=true to the connection string to keep failing at startup\n\t\t\t- appendGuidToConsumerGroup: an instance deletes its own consumer group when it stops, and on start deletes groups of stopped instances whose consumers were idle for more than an hour, so old groups no longer pile up, keep entries in the stream or cause entries to be processed again\n\t\t\t- Performance: the worker reads the next batch right away while entries are queued and pauses only when the stream is empty, which lifts the previous limit of about 100 entries per second per consumer with the default BatchSize\n\t\t\t- The Redis stream entry format is unchanged; entries written by 1.0.x are read by 1.1.0 and the other way round",
